0192 | A {\em vertex cut} of a graph $G$ is a set $X \subset V(G)$ such that $G - X$ is a disconnected graph.
0193 | Let $G$ and $H$ be two graphs for which $V(G) \cap V(H)$ is a clique.  We call the graph with vertex set $V(G) \cup V(H)$ and edge set $E(G) \cup E(H)$ the {\em clique sum} of $G$ and $H$; this is also called a {\em clique pasting} of $G$ and $H$.
0194 | 
0195 | The following statements are well known to be equivalent:
0196 | 
0197 | 	\begin{compactitem}
0198 | 	\item $G$ is chordal.
0199 | 	\item Every minimal vertex cut of every induced subgraph of $G$ is a clique \cite{D61}.
0200 | 	\item $G$ admits a perfect elimination ordering \cite{D61, FG65}.
0201 | 	\end{compactitem}
0202 | 
0203 | 
0204 | A consequence of the first characterization is that $G$ is chordal if and only if $G$ can obtained from two chordal graphs $G_1$ and $G_2$, with $V(G_1) \subsetneq V(G)$ and $V(G_2) \subsetneq V(G)$, via clique pasting.
0205 | We will make use of all three characterizations of chordal graphs in this paper.
